Q: How to achieve a nice-looking scrolling overflow on preformatted text in without fixed width? A: <p>You need to put the <code>overflow-x: auto;</code> on a parent, not on the element itself. Tables are also special when it comes to layout. The table, as a whole, will often adapt to its content, so it can become wider than you want. So the best method is to set the width of the table cells. Combining these two things gives you something like this:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;
        &lt;div style="max-width: 20vw; overflow-x: auto;"&gt;
          &lt;pre&gt;Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... Very long lines go here... &lt;/pre&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>Note that I introduces an extra <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element in the cell to handle the overflow. This will avoid setting any of the properties the cell needs to have.</p>

Q: Set Max JSON Length in .NET Core A: <p>There's no direct equivalent of the property in new .NET.</p>
<p>But it's quite straightforward to mimic such behaviour:</p>
<h2>Incoming traffic</h2>
<p>For requests bodies it's simple to define cap for size:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>builder.Services.Configure&lt;KestrelServerOptions&gt;(
    o =&gt; o.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 100);
</code></pre>
<h2>Outgoing traffic</h2>
<p>Define middleware that would check the body after executing <code>next()</code> callback:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class JsonLengthMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
{
    const int MaxResponseBodySize = 1024;

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var originalBody = context.Response.Body;
        using var buffer = new MemoryStream();
        context.Response.Body = buffer;

        await next(context);

        if (buffer.Length &gt; MaxResponseBodySize)
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                $&quot;Response body exceeded limit: {buffer.Length} bytes (max {MaxResponseBodySize})&quot;);

        buffer.Seek(0, SeekOrigin.Begin);
        await buffer.CopyToAsync(originalBody);
        context.Response.Body = originalBody;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then register it with <code>app.UseMiddleware&lt;JsonLengthMiddleware&gt;();</code> - <strong>just make sure to register it at the end!</strong> Otherwise some, if there is some middleware after, it can still modify the returned body.</p>
